{"name":"River Shark","hex":"#dfdcd5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"river shark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any shark of the genus Glyphis (family Carcharhinidae)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river_shark"}],"entries":[{"title":"River shark","blocks":[{"text":"Glyphis is a genus in the family Carcharhinidae, commonly known as the river sharks. They live in rivers or coastal regions in and around southeast Asia, and parts of Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Speartooth_shark_melbourne.jpg/330px-Speartooth_shark_melbourne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_shark"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River"},{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/330px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Glyphis_gangeticus.jpg/250px-Glyphis_gangeticus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Glyphis_gangeticus.jpg/330px-Glyphis_gangeticus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Glyphis_gangeticus.jpg/250px-Glyphis_gangeticus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlyphis_gangeticus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Glyphis_garricki_csiro-nfc.jpg/250px-Glyphis_garricki_csiro-nfc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Glyphis_garricki_csiro-nfc.jpg/500px-Glyphis_garricki_csiro-nfc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip